4️⃣ Authorization Flow

After authentication, server checks user roles/permissions stored in JWT claims

Example in Spring Security:

@GetMapping("/admin")
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
public String adminPage() {
    return "Admin Content";
}

Users without ADMIN role get 403 Forbidden
Authorization determines what an authenticated user is allowed to do. It always happens after authentication and can be role-based, attribute-based, or policy-based.

1. Definition

Authorization = Access control

Confirms permissions / roles of a user to perform an action

Ensures that even authenticated users cannot access resources they aren’t allowed to

Authentication = “Who are you?”
Authorization = “What can you do?”

2. How It Works with JWT

User is authenticated → server has verified credentials

JWT contains claims → roles, permissions, or attributes

Server checks claims for the requested resource/action

Access decision:

Allowed → request proceeds

Denied → server returns 403 Forbidden

3. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Example

JWT Payload:

{
  "sub": "user123",
  "role": "ADMIN"
}

Admin can access /admin/dashboard

Customer cannot access /admin/dashboard → 403

Spring Security Example:

@GetMapping("/admin/dashboard")
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
public String adminDashboard() {
    return "Admin Content";
}

Checks JWT role claim

Denies access if role does not match

4. Attribute-Based / Fine-Grained Authorization

Decisions can also be made based on attributes, e.g.:

{
  "sub": "user123",
  "role": "CUSTOMER",
  "accountId": "ACC987654"
}

Customer can access /account/{accountId}/balance only if accountId in JWT matches the requested account

Prevents access to other users’ accounts

Spring Security Example:

@GetMapping("/account/{accountId}/balance")
public String getBalance(@PathVariable String accountId, @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails user) {
    if (!user.getAccountId().equals(accountId)) {
        throw new AccessDeniedException("Forbidden");
    }
    return "Balance: $1000";
}
